# Cloudflare Pages proxy — PART 6: same-site cookie fix.
#
# Problem this solves: frontend (Cloudflare Pages) and backend (Vercel) were
# on two different domains, so the Flask session cookie was a THIRD-PARTY
# cookie from the browser's point of view. Safari blocks third-party cookies
# by default, Brave blocks them by default, Firefox's "Strict" tracking
# protection blocks them, and some users have Chrome set to block them too.
# For anyone on those browsers/settings, login would appear to succeed
# ("Welcome back") but the cookie was silently dropped, so the very next
# page load looked logged-out and bounced back to /login.
#
# Fix: every request to /api/* on THIS (frontend) domain is transparently
# proxied to the Vercel backend. The browser only ever talks to one domain,
# so the cookie becomes a normal first-party cookie — no browser blocks it,
# no SameSite=None dance needed, no architecture change (Vercel backend +
# Cloudflare frontend stays exactly as-is).
#
# :splat carries everything matched by the trailing * — e.g. a request to
# /api/login here becomes a request to /login on the Vercel origin, a
# request to /api/sync becomes /sync, etc. 200 (not 301/302) makes this a
# transparent proxy/rewrite instead of a redirect, so the browser URL bar
# and BASE_API_URL logic in config.js never see the real backend domain.
/api/*  https://daily-submission-tracker-ciyc.vercel.app/:splat  200
